        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains July 15 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 2 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For July 15 in music history, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes July 15.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Shadow Dancing" had the most weeks, with seven, and "It's Too Late" and "Bad Girls" had five each.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week, and only "Undercover Angel" had just that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} were in the final week of their runs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only July 15 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the July 15 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called July 15.</p>
        <p>This July 15 in music history page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains July 15 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>All ten runs were unbroken, so "week of run" counts the weeks since the song first reached number one.</p>

        <h2 id="early">July 15 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Randy Newman Cover, a Carole King Breakup Song and a Slab Fork Anthem</h2>
        <p>The first three Julys of the decade brought a Three Dog Night cover, a Carole King album track that took over the radio and a Bill Withers song about friendship.</p>

        <h3>1970: "Mama Told Me (Not to Come)" by Three Dog Night</h3>
        <p>Randy Newman wrote "Mama Told Me (Not to Come)" in 1966, and Wikipedia says it was written for Eric Burdon's first solo album.</p>
        <p>Eric Burdon &amp; The Animals cut the first recording, and it appeared on their 1967 album Eric Is Here after a single planned for September 1966 was withdrawn.</p>
        <p>Newman released his own version on the 1970 album 12 Songs, with mid-tempo piano and Ry Cooder's slide guitar.</p>
        <p>Three Dog Night's cover topped the Hot 100 for two weeks in July 1970.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows those two weeks as July 11 and July 18, 1970.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 11 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More on the band is on the <a href="/blog/artists/three-dog-night-songs/">Three Dog Night songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "It's Too Late" by Carole King</h3>
        <p>Toni Stern wrote the lyrics and Carole King wrote the music for "It's Too Late."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Stern later told author Sheila Weller that she wrote the lyrics in a single day, after her relationship with James Taylor ended.</p>
        <p>The same page notes that this conflicts with obituaries written by her husband, Jerry Rounds.</p>
        <p>It was recorded at A&amp;M Studios in Hollywood, produced by Lou Adler and released as a single by Ode Records in April 1971.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly Hot 100 file lists the number one as "It's Too Late / I Feel the Earth Move."</p>
        <p>It shows five weeks at number one, from June 19 to July 17, 1971.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the fourth of five.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/carole-king-its-too-late/">Carole King It's Too Late</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Lean on Me" by Bill Withers</h3>
        <p>Bill Withers wrote and recorded "Lean on Me," and it was released in April 1972 as the lead single from his album Still Bill.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says he drew on his childhood in the coal mining town of Slab Fork, West Virginia, and wrote it after moving to Los Angeles and missing the strong community ethic of his hometown.</p>
        <p>Members of the Watts 103rd Street Rhythm Band played on the session, and a string section was added.</p>
        <p>Withers recalled that the title phrase came while he was idly playing a small piano he had bought.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from July 8 to July 22, 1972.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the second of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 7 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">July 15 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Preston Piano Hit, a Disco Landmark and a Song Sedaka Wrote First</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a Billy Preston number one, a KC and the Sunshine Band production and a Captain &amp; Tennille song with a Neil Sedaka past.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Will It Go Round in Circles" by Billy Preston</h3>
        <p>Wikipedia says Billy Preston and Bruce Fisher wrote "Will It Go Round in Circles."</p>
        <p>It came from Preston's 1972 album Music Is My Life and was released as a single in March 1973.</p>
        <p>Preston produced it and played piano and sang, and bassist Louis Johnson is among the musicians listed.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it sold over a million copies.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, July 7 and July 14, 1973.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 8 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Rock Your Baby" by George McCrae</h3>
        <p>Harry Wayne Casey and Richard Finch of KC and the Sunshine Band wrote and produced "Rock Your Baby."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it an early landmark recording of disco.</p>
        <p>The backing track was recorded in 45 minutes as a demo, with Casey on keyboards, Finch on bass and drums and Jerome Smith on electric guitar.</p>
        <p>The track was not originally meant for George McCrae, but he was in the studio at the time and added his falsetto vocals.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, July 13 and July 20, 1974.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 38 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The writers' own group is on the <a href="/blog/artists/kc-and-the-sunshine-band-songs/">KC and the Sunshine Band songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Love Will Keep Us Together" by Captain &amp; Tennille</h3>
        <p>Neil Sedaka and Howard Greenfield wrote "Love Will Keep Us Together."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Sedaka first recorded it in spring 1973 at Strawberry Studios in Stockport, England, for his album The Tra-La Days Are Over.</p>
        <p>Sedaka has admitted borrowing the main chord progression from the Beach Boys' "Do It Again."</p>
        <p>The Captain &amp; Tennille version spent four weeks at number one, starting June 21, 1975.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with four weeks from June 21 to July 12, 1975.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the fourth and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="late">July 15 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Happy Hour Menu, a Nocturnal Novelette, a Gibb Family Run and a Police Stop</h2>
        <p>The last four Julys brought the longest run on the list and a Donna Summer number one.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Afternoon Delight" by Starland Vocal Band</h3>
        <p>Band member Bill Danoff wrote "Afternoon Delight."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was inspired by a happy hour menu at Clyde's in Georgetown, Washington, D.C., titled "Afternoon Delights."</p>
        <p>Danoff said he wanted something fun that hinted at sex, not an all-out sex song.</p>
        <p>It was recorded in November 1975, released in April 1976 and produced by Milt Okun.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, July 10 and July 17, 1976.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 12 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "Undercover Angel" by Alan O'Day</h3>
        <p>Alan O'Day wrote "Undercover Angel."</p>
        <p>Pacific Records released it as a single in February 1977, with "Just You" on the B-side.</p>
        <p>It was later included on his 1977 album Appetizers, and Steve Barri and Michael Omartian produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says O'Day called it a "nocturnal novelette."</p>
        <p>It reached number one in the US before an album to support it was released.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of July 9, 1977.</p>
        <p>The song is number 9 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Shadow Dancing" by Andy Gibb</h3>
        <p>Andy Gibb wrote "Shadow Dancing" with his brothers Barry, Maurice and Robin, and Albhy Galuten co-wrote and produced it.</p>
        <p>Barry Gibb arranged it, and Andy said, "we all wrote it, the four of us."</p>
        <p>It was recorded at Criteria Studios in Miami between December 1977 and February 1978.</p>
        <p>RSO Records released it in April 1978.</p>
        <p>In July 1978, Barry, Robin and Maurice joined Andy onstage at a Miami concert.</p>
        <p>It was the first time all four brothers performed together in concert.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it spent seven weeks at number one, from June 17 to July 29, 1978.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, so the chart week that contains July 15 was the fifth of seven.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Bad Girls" by Donna Summer</h3>
        <p>Donna Summer co-wrote "Bad Girls" with Bruce Sudano, Joe "Bean" Esposito and Edward Hokenson of Brooklyn Dreams.</p>
        <p>Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the idea came after one of Summer's assistants was insulted by a police officer who thought she was a prostitute.</p>
        <p>It was recorded in 1979 at Rusk Sound Studios in Los Angeles.</p>
        <p>Casablanca founder Neil Bogart wanted Cher to have the song, but Summer refused.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows five weeks at number one, from July 14 to August 11, 1979.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains July 15 was the first of five.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/bad-girls/">Bad Girls</a> page.</p>

